'''Dafar Hoose''' (Burawi: ''Dafar Hoose'', meaning "Lower Dafar"), officially the '''Dafar Hoose Rural Administration''', is a [[Administrative divisions of Burawa|rural administration]] in the [[Dafar]] cultural region of western [[Burawa]]. With an area of 17,754.04 km² and a population of 561,402 as of 2026, it is characterised by its agricultural economy and its unique geographic position surrounding the [[Sago-Dare Metropolitan Administration]].

The administration is bordered by [[Dafar Sare Rural Administration|Dafar Sare]] to the northeast, [[Badweyn Rural Administration|Badweyn]] to the south, and [[Iskhal Dibadda Rural Administration|Iskhal Dibadda]] to the west. The seat of government is the town of [[Dafar Hoose (town)|Dafar Hoose]].

Dafar Hoose is the southern stronghold of the [[Sago Revolutionary Party]], which dominates the administration's politics alongside neighbouring Dafar Sare. The region is known for its agricultural production and its relationship with the enclosed metropolitan area of Sago-Dare.

== Geography ==

Dafar Hoose occupies the southwestern portion of the [[Dafar]] region in western [[Burawa]]. The terrain consists primarily of rolling agricultural land interspersed with valleys and low hills. The region's fertile soils support its agricultural economy.

Dafar Hoose borders [[Dafar Sare Rural Administration|Dafar Sare]] to the northeast, [[Badweyn Rural Administration|Badweyn]] to the south, and [[Iskhal Dibadda Rural Administration|Iskhal Dibadda]] to the west. The administration completely surrounds the [[Sago-Dare Metropolitan Administration]] on its landward sides, with the metropolitan area's multiple municipalities forming an enclave within Dafar Hoose.

