== History ==

=== Formation ===

The Dafar Hoose Rural Administration was constituted in 1967 under the [[Administration Reorganization Act (Burawa)|Administration Reorganization Act]], which reorganised Burawa's administrative divisions into the current three-tier system. The administration was carved from the historic [[Sago]] region.

The name "Dafar Hoose" derives from the Burawi words for "Lower Dafar," reflecting the administration's position in the southern portion of the Dafar region.

=== Relationship with Sago-Dare ===

The enclosure of the Sago-Dare Metropolitan Administration within Dafar Hoose has created a complex administrative relationship. The rural administration serves as the agricultural hinterland for the metropolitan area, while the city provides markets and services for the rural population. This relationship has been a source of both cooperation and tension, particularly regarding land use, water rights, and transportation infrastructure.

=== Political developments ===

Historically, Dafar Hoose was a stronghold of the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]], consistent with the broader Dafar region's conservative and agrarian political character. This began to change in the 2020s with the rise of the [[Sago Revolutionary Party]], which expanded from its base in neighbouring Dafar Sare to capture Dafar Hoose's political institutions.

== Government and politics ==

As a rural administration, Dafar Hoose is governed by an administrator who serves a six-year term. The administrator is nominated by the senator and confirmed by a vote of the communes within the administration. The incumbent administrator is [[Mahad Cusmaan]] of the [[Sago Revolutionary Party]], appointed in 2022.

=== Senate representation ===

{{main|Senate of Burawa}}

Dafar Hoose holds one seat in the [[Senate of Burawa]]. The incumbent senator is [[Guleed Dheere]] of the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]], first elected in 2018.

=== Lower House representation ===

