=== Political culture ===

Dafar Hoose shares the regionalist political culture of neighbouring Dafar Sare, characterised by attachment to the Sago identity and grievances regarding perceived neglect by the national government. The [[Sago Revolutionary Party]] has capitalised on these sentiments, expanding its support from Dafar Sare into Dafar Hoose.

== Economy ==

The economy of Dafar Hoose is predominantly agricultural. The fertile lands support the cultivation of grain, vegetables, and fruit. Livestock husbandry, particularly cattle and sheep, is also significant. The region's proximity to the Sago-Dare metropolitan area provides ready markets for agricultural products.

The administration has limited industrial development, with small-scale food processing and agricultural services constituting the primary non-agricultural employment. Many residents commute to the enclosed Sago-Dare metropolitan area for employment in urban industries and services.

== Demographics ==

The population of Dafar Hoose is 561,402 as of 2026, dispersed across the administration's 17,754.04 km² territory. The population density of 31.6 persons per km² reflects the predominance of agricultural land use.

The majority of residents are ethnically [[Burawi people|Burawi]], with small minorities of [[Kijuran people|Kijuran]] and other groups. The primary language is [[Burawi language|Burawi]], with regional dialects characteristic of the Dafar region.

